# Green p1 task 1.19 (adversarial fixes ADV-1 to ADV-11)

The committed Red change f014ee8 (adversarial.test.ts) sits inside
phase_base_sha..HEAD. HEAD at start was
8eafbc3255d6f012aa23b119961c2e5a8ef6da40, the role_base_sha. Dirty paths
.opencode/ and tech-debt.md were not touched.

Fixes (all in harness/model-guide/):

- publish.ts (1cc5657): the docs root is lstatted and a root symlink gives
  `publish: bad docs root`, exit 2, before any drift walk or copy (ADV-1).
  The record must hold a non-empty `approved_by:` line and a non-empty
  `date:` line, else `publish: bad approval record`, exit 2 (ADV-2). An
  unreadable docs root prints `publish: drift .` and no empty path (ADV-5).
  A preview base that is not a JSON object, or a `docs` value that is not an
  object, gives `publish: bad preview base`, exit 2, no stack trace (ADV-10).
  Each approved file is staged in its target folder before the first rename;
  a destination that cannot be staged gives `publish: write <path>`, exit 2,
  and the run removes its temp files, so no partial set stays (ADV-11).
- preview.sh (f3d5843): one Python scan of docs/ replaces the find calls. A
  symlink or another non-regular entry (a FIFO) gives
  `preview: refused path <absolute path>`, exit 2, before any write (ADV-4).
  An unreadable entry gives `preview: unreadable <path relative to the
  repo>`, exit 2; an unreadable docs root prints `docs`; an unreadable
  folder no longer ends the run with a find error (ADV-3). The header names
  both refusal lines and the `docs` value.
- real-work.ts (b7e24da): a missing input path or a path that is not a
  regular file gives `input-check: input`, exit 2; text that is not JSON
  gives `input-check: json`; a non-object root, a bad coverage block, and a
  row with no string route give `input-check: schema`, all with no stack
  trace and no write (ADV-6). The output copies only the coverage keys of
  spec EV4 plus `unknown_runs` and `other_runs`, so an extra key such as
  `private_note` reaches no public file (ADV-7). Work types and rating axes
  are built with Object.fromEntries, so a name such as `__proto__` stays a
  row (ADV-8). Every count is a non-negative safe integer, and each sum is
  checked, so a negative or huge count gives `input-check: schema` (ADV-9).
  The header names the `input` and `json` lines and the schema row rules.

Brief vs tests: no difference. The tests pin the exact refusal texts and
were followed.

